    modifiedTime: "2026-11-05",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "cybex treadmill",
      "treadmill cybex",
      "cybex treadmills",
      "cybex 770t treadmill",
      "cybex r series treadmill",
      "used cybex treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Most Common Used: Cybex 770T", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/brands/">Brands</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buyer’s Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Cybex Treadmills: What to Know Before Buying One Used</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Cybex treadmills were a fixture of American gyms for decades, and the 770T and R Series still fill the used and remanufactured market. But Cybex no longer sells a new treadmill: Life Fitness, which bought the company in 2016, now lists the R Series as discontinued and carries Cybex designs forward under its own names. Here is what that means for a buyer, what the figures are, which old models were recalled, and how to buy a used Cybex well.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 5, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#cybex-770t"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41QUtPP1SBL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Cybex 770T" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#matrix-t5x"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41V75z6TXyL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Matrix T5x" width="500" height="416" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#life-fitness-t3"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41xQfD1DGZL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Life Fitness T3" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Cybex 770T · Matrix T5x · Life Fitness T3</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Search for a Cybex treadmill and you will find plenty of them: remanufactured 770Ts with
          touchscreen consoles, R Series machines out of hotel gyms, and older 500-series units on local
          marketplaces. What you will not find is a new one. Cybex International was bought by Life
          Fitness in 2016, Life Fitness's US catalogue marks the Cybex R Series treadmill as
          discontinued, and its Cybex page now presents the brand as a legacy carried forward in Life
          Fitness and Hammer Strength products. That does not make a used Cybex a bad buy — these were
          well-built club machines — but it changes the questions you need to ask. This guide sets out
          what Cybex treadmills are, the figures for the two models most often for sale, the old recalls,
          what Life Fitness still supports, and how to buy one.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Most Common Used:</span> <a href="#cybex-770t">Cybex 770T</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon to the R Series:</span> <a href="#matrix-t5x">Matrix T5x</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Buy New Instead:</span> <a href="#life-fitness-t3">Life Fitness T3</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">New on a Budget:</span> <a href="#life-fitness-run-cx">Life Fitness Run CX</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          5 November 2026 we checked Life Fitness's US Cybex pages (its Cybex brand page and the Cybex R
          Series treadmill catalogue page), the Cybex 770T owner's manual specification page, Life Fitness
          support articles on discontinued Cybex service (titles and summaries in search results; the
          articles themselves refused our requests), remanufactured-equipment listings from Build A Gym
          and, through search results, Fitness Superstore, CTX Home Gyms and Progym Supply, a Cybex R
          Series sell sheet and review summarised in search results, Life Fitness's US store for the
          Club Series+ and Run CX, and the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database. On
          9 November 2026 we replaced the R Series and Club Series+ cards with the Matrix T5x and Life
          Fitness T3, which are sold on Amazon; their figures are those checked for our Matrix T5x review
          and non-folding treadmill guide.
          Figures on the product cards are those on which Cybex or Life Fitness documents and at least
          one independent source agree; conflicts are set out in the text and kept off the cards.
          Used and remanufactured prices come from individual listings, several of which we could only
          see in search results, and change constantly. We have not tested any Cybex treadmill. As an
          Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying purchases; that does not change what we say.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Can You Still Buy a Cybex Treadmill? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-is-cybex">Who Was Cybex, and Who Owns It Now?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#models">Cybex Treadmill Models You Will See for Sale</a></li>
            <li><a href="#recalls">Cybex Treadmill Recalls</a></li>
            <li><a href="#buying-used">How to Buy a Used or Remanufactured Cybex</a></li>
            <li><a href="#home-install">Putting a Cybex in Your Home</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">Used Cybex or New Life Fitness?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Can You Still Buy a Cybex Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Not new. Cybex treadmills are now sold only used or remanufactured. A remanufactured
            770T or R Series from a seller who gives a written warranty can be a sound way to get a club
            treadmill for about $3,000; for similar money, a new Life Fitness Run CX comes with a far longer
            warranty.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            On 5 November 2026 Life Fitness's US catalogue page for the Cybex R Series treadmill — the last
            treadmill Cybex designed — was marked "Discontinued", and Life Fitness's Cybex page listed no
            current Cybex-branded products at all. It describes Cybex as a brand "acquired by Life Fitness in
            2016" whose designs carry on under other names: the Cybex Arc Trainer as the Life Fitness Arc
            Trainer, Cybex Eagle strength as the Life Fitness Insignia Series, and Cybex plate-loaded machines
            under Hammer Strength. There is no Life Fitness treadmill sold as the Cybex replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cybex treadmills are still everywhere on the used market because gyms bought them in large
            numbers and are now replacing them. Remanufactured 770Ts were listed between about $2,795 and
            $3,899 in search results on the day we checked, and a remanufactured R Series at $3,095 (sold
            out). Those are reasonable prices for a club treadmill, provided you get a real warranty, have a
            dedicated circuit and know which model you are buying. We have not tested any Cybex treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-cybex" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Was Cybex, and Who Owns It Now?</h2>
<p>
            Cybex International was a Massachusetts maker of commercial strength and cardio equipment,
            best known for selectorized strength machines, the Arc Trainer and its club treadmills. It also
            sold treadmills under the Trotter name, which appears in its older recall records. Life Fitness
            acquired Cybex in 2016, and Life Fitness itself was sold by Brunswick Corporation to KPS Capital
            Partners in 2019. Life Fitness now sells Life Fitness, Hammer Strength and Indoor Cycling Group
            equipment; Cybex survives as a name on older machines and in support documents.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Not the stroller company</h3>
          <p>
            Cybex is also the name of a German maker of car seats and strollers, Cybex GmbH, which is
            unrelated. If a recall or a support page mentions strollers or car seats, it is about that
            company. Everything on this page refers to Cybex International, the fitness company.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Life Fitness still supports</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness's support site hosts Cybex manuals and assembly instructions, including for the R
            Series, and a series of notices ending support for older Cybex treadmills. Their titles, which we
            could read in search results, cover the 300T, 600T, 700T, 710T and 685 (support ending from April
            2009), the 450T, 500T, 515T and 520T, the 540 CXT AC drive treadmills, the Q Series, and the
            discontinuation of Cybex treadmill lower-controller repair. The articles themselves would not
            load for us, so check them directly for exact dates and models. The practical point is that the
            older the Cybex, the more likely a failed electronic board must be sourced from a third-party
            parts supplier rather than Life Fitness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cybex Treadmill Models You Will See for Sale</h2>
<p>
            Cybex used a number-and-letter scheme: a series number with "T" for treadmill (770T, 625T, 530T),
            and, for the R Series, a console name (50L or 70T). The table lists the models most often for sale,
            with what we found. The product cards below cover the Cybex 770T, the Cybex most often listed on
            Amazon, and the nearest alternatives sold on Amazon. The Cybex R Series and the Life Fitness Club
            Series+ are sold through remanufacturers, Life Fitness and dealers rather than on Amazon, so the
            cards show a Matrix T5x club treadmill in the R Series's place and the new Life Fitness T3 and Run
            CX as the buy-new options; the R Series figures are in the text below.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it is</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Note for buyers</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">R Series (50L / 70T)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Last Cybex treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Discontinued; youngest used units</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">770T, 790T</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Top club treadmills, LED or E3 console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Most common remanufactured Cybex</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">625T, 750T, 751T</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Club treadmills</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check parts availability</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">445T-550T</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Older models</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">115 V units recalled in 2008; confirm repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400T, 410T, Trotter 510-535</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Early models</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Recalled in 2003 and again in 2007; avoid</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 770T: figures and conflicts</h3>
          <p>
            The 770T owner's manual lists 0.5 to 12.4 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 400 lb maximum user, an
            assembled length of 84 inches, a width of 35.6 inches, a height of 62.5 inches and a product weight
            of 410 lb. Retailer listings agree on the speed and user weight, add a 5 HP peak AC drive motor and
            a 1,000 lb-thrust incline motor, and give 82.5 by 36 by 59 inches and 500 lb. The difference may
            reflect the console fitted or a <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>shipping weight</a>, but we cannot tell, so neither figure is on the
            card. We found no reliable running-surface figure; measure the belt on any unit you view.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The R Series: figures and conflicts</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness's catalogue page for the R Series lists a 4.0 HP continuous, 8.0 HP peak AC motor,
            400 lb, an 8.5-inch step-up and a dedicated 20-amp circuit at 120 V. It also gives two different
            surface sizes: a 22 by 60-inch belt and a 21 by 60-inch running area. That is not necessarily a
            mistake — the belt is wider than the usable surface between the side rails — and Cybex's sell sheet
            and an independent review both give 21 by 60 inches. Speed (14 mph) and incline (0 to 15%) come
            from the sell sheet and that review; Life Fitness's page did not list them.
          </p>
        </div>
