        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cybex Treadmill Recalls</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Cybex on 5 November
            2026. Three recalls involve treadmills. None covers the 770T or the R Series.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>October 2008:</strong> about 19,000 Cybex 445T, 455T, 530T, 450T, 500T, 515T, 520T and
            550T treadmills, 115 V versions only, which could speed up unexpectedly and cause a fall. The 220 V
            versions were not included.</li>
            <li><strong>October 2003:</strong> 33,719 Cybex 400T and 410T and Trotter 510, 525 and 535
            treadmills, plus a CXT+ model that carried neither brand.</li>
            <li><strong>October 2007:</strong> a further recall of about 4,700 of those 2003 treadmills that
            had already been repaired, for a fire hazard.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Cybex's other recalls cover strength equipment (weight benches, an arm curl and a <a href="/best-leg-press-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>leg press</a>), and
            a 2010 recall of Cybex strollers was issued by the unrelated stroller company. If you are offered a
            Cybex from the 400 or 500 series, ask for proof that the recall repair was done, or walk away; there
            is little reason to buy a recalled 20-year-old treadmill when 770Ts are plentiful.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Buy a Used or Remanufactured Cybex</h2>
<p>
            Because no new Cybex is sold, every purchase is a used one. The seller and the paperwork matter more
            than the model.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Remanufactured, refurbished or as-is</h3>
          <p>
            "Remanufactured" should mean the treadmill was stripped down, cleaned, given new wear parts and
            tested. Build A Gym's R Series listing describes exactly that: fully disassembled, deep cleaned,
            repaired or re-parted to factory specification, tested under load, with belts, cables and bearings
            rebuilt or replaced. "Refurbished" and "serviced" can mean much less. Ask what was replaced — belt,
            deck, <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>motor brushes</a> or bearings, rollers — and get it in writing. Our <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills guide</a> explains the grades.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Remanufacturers give their own warranty, not Life Fitness's. In the listings we saw, that was one
            year of parts and labour as standard, with extensions sold separately: Build A Gym offered a second
            year for $399 and a third for $749, and Progym Supply's listing in search results mentioned three
            years of parts and labour. A seller who will not put a warranty in writing is selling as-is.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Check the listing against the machine</h3>
          <p>
            Remanufacturers' listings are not always careful. Build A Gym's R Series 70T listing called the
            machine a treadmill in its title and a "Total Body Arc Trainer" with moving arms in its
            description, and gave both a dedicated 20-amp circuit and "a standard household outlet" as the
            power requirement. Ask for photos of the serial-number plate and the console, and confirm the
            electrical requirement against the manual.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to inspect</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Run it at your real speeds and through the full incline range, listening for motor and roller noise.</li>
            <li>Look at the belt edges and the deck under the belt for wear; ask whether the deck was flipped or replaced.</li>
            <li>Check the console: a touchscreen with TV or entertainment features from the 2010s may have little
            left to stream to, and a failed entertainment console is an expensive part.</li>
            <li>Ask for the hour meter or odometer reading if the console shows one.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> and <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a> guide cover the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Putting a Cybex in Your Home</h2>
<p>
            A Cybex is a gym machine, and it brings gym requirements into a house. Check three things before
            buying, not after.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Circuit.</strong> Life Fitness lists a dedicated 120 V, 20 A circuit for the R Series
            in North America, and the 770T manual and retailers call for a dedicated circuit. Most household
            outlets are on 15-amp circuits; an electrician fitting a 20-amp circuit is part of the cost.</li>
            <li><strong>Space and weight.</strong> The 770T is 82.5 to 84 inches long and weighs 410 to 500 lb
            depending on the source. Add safety clearance behind and to the sides.</li>
            <li><strong>Delivery.</strong> Ask whether the price includes delivery, stairs and installation;
            many remanufacturers charge for these separately.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to putting a gym treadmill in a home</a> covers circuits, floors and access routes for any commercial treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Used Cybex or New Life Fitness?</h2>
<p>
            For about $3,000, a remanufactured 770T or R Series gets you a club AC drive and a 400 lb rating,
            with typically one year of a seller's warranty. On 5 November 2026 the new <a href="/life-fitness-run-cx-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness Run CX</a> was $3,919 in Life Fitness's <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Black Friday</a> sale, with a lifetime frame, a 10-year motor term,
            7 years on parts, delivery and installation, and no 20-amp circuit. For most home users that is the
            better buy. The used Cybex wins only if you specifically want a club AC treadmill, will put
            gym-level hours on it, or can buy one well under $2,500.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want the club build new, the <a href="/life-fitness-club-series-plus-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness Club Series+</a> has the R Series's 4.0 HP continuous AC rating and 400 lb capacity on a standard outlet, from
            $6,079 on the same day. Other club brands sold used and remanufactured include <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a>, <a href="/star-trac-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Star Trac</a> and <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix</a>; the same buying rules
            apply. Our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used treadmill guide</a> sets out when used makes sense.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is Cybex on Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            We found no new Cybex treadmill on Amazon US, as you would expect for a discontinued brand.
            Amazon does carry a Cybex 770T listing, which the 770T card links to; check the seller, the
            condition and the warranty before you order, as you would with any remanufacturer, and ask for
            the list of replaced parts. Searches for the R Series mostly return parts rather than a whole
            machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Cybex Treadmill</h2>
          <p>A used Cybex can be a good machine at a good price. These are the ways buyers end up with neither.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a recalled 400- or 500-series Cybex</h3>
              <p>About 19,000 115 V Cybex 445T to 550T treadmills were recalled in 2008 because they could speed up unexpectedly, and the 400T and 410T in 2003. These are now around two decades old. Unless the seller can prove the repair, skip them; remanufactured 770Ts and R Series units are easy to find.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting a remanufacturer’s listing without checking</h3>
              <p>One R Series listing we read described an Arc Trainer in its text and gave two different power requirements. Ask for photos of the serial plate and console, check the model against Life Fitness’s manual, and get the list of replaced parts in writing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the circuit</h3>
              <p>Club treadmills like the R Series need a dedicated 20-amp circuit, which most rooms do not have. Price the electrician into the purchase. A $3,000 used Cybex plus a new circuit and delivery can cost as much as a new Life Fitness Run CX that plugs into a normal outlet.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for an old entertainment console</h3>
              <p>E3 and 70T touchscreen consoles add hundreds of dollars to used prices, but their TV and app features date from the 2010s and may have little left to connect to. A simpler LED console has less to fail. Pay for the touchscreen only if it works fully and you will use it.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Cybex no longer sells treadmills: Life Fitness bought the brand in 2016
          and lists the R Series, the last Cybex treadmill, as discontinued.</strong> A remanufactured 770T
          or R Series at around $3,000, with a written warranty and a list of replaced parts, is a fair way
          to get a club AC treadmill — if you have a dedicated circuit and avoid the recalled 400- and
          500-series models.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most home buyers the new <a href="/life-fitness-run-cx-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Life Fitness Run CX</a> is the better use of similar money, with a lifetime frame warranty, delivery and installation, and a
          standard outlet. Buy the used Cybex when you specifically want the club machine.
          </p>
        </div>

